¶
'''unseel<sup>2</sup>†''' (un-sēl′), ''v. t.'' [< ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''seel''<sup>2</sup>.] To open, as the eyes of a hawk which have been seeled; restore the sight of; enlighten.
{{smaller block|Are your eyes yet ''unseeled?'' dare they look day<br>In the dull face? {{right|''B. Jonson'', Catiline, i. 1.}}}}

'''unseeliness†''' (un-sē′li-nes), ''n.'' Wretchedness; unblessedness.
{{smaller block|I desire gretly that shrewes losten sone thilke ''unselynysses.'' {{right|''Chaucer'', Boëthius, iv. prose 4.}}}}

'''unseely†''' (un-sē′li), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unsely'', ''unselig'', ''unceli'', ''unsælig'', < AS. ''unsēlig'', ''unsælig'' (= OHG. ''unsālig'', MHG. ''unsælic'', ''unsælec'' = Icel. ''ūsælligr'' = Dan. ''usalig''), unhappy, < ''un-'', not, + ''sēlig'', happy: see ''seely''.] Unhappy; unfortunate; unsuccessful.
{{smaller block|“Unhardy is ''unseely'',” thus men sayth. {{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 290.}}}}

'''unseem†''' (un-sēm′), ''v. i.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''seem''<sup>1</sup>.] Not to seem. ''Shak.'', L. L. L., ii. 1. 156.

'''unseemliness''' (un-sēm′li-nes), ''n.'' The character of being unseemly; uncomeliness; indecency; indecorum; impropriety. ''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity.

'''unseemly''' (un-sēm′li), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unsemly'' (= Icel. ''ūsæmiligr''); < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''seemly''.] Not seemly; not fit or becoming; uncomely; unbecoming; indecent; improper.
